        "<p>To calibrate the distances and mass distributions of these stellar populations across galactic scales, astronomers utilize statistical distance indicators. By measuring the mean proper motions of distant stars relative to the solar motion, the method of <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/secular-parallax\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>Secular Parallax</strong></a> establishes an absolute distance scale. In the hot stellar core where these masses are concentrated, the nuclear fusion of hydrogen is governed by the probability of nuclei tunneling through <a href=\"/physics/subtopic/coulomb-barrier\" class=\"subtopic-link\"><strong>The Coulomb Barrier</strong></a>. This quantum tunneling probability \\( P_C \\approx e^{-2\\pi \\eta} \\) is highly sensitive to the central density and temperature profiles predicted by the mass conservation equation. This subatomic mapping ensures that the calculated energy generation rates are highly consistent with the local mass-energy conservation, showing how local nuclear physics and global stellar structure are compared. By cross-referencing these statistical parallaxes with the physical constraints of nuclear fusion, cosmologists can verify the density profiles of nearby stellar groups, ensuring that the theoretical integrations of mass conservation are aligned with observational stellar kinematics.</p>"
